{"chapter":"1","chapter_id":"dn-ch1","document_id":"dn","id":"dn-ch1-thm-1.1","kind":"theorem","label":"1.1","name":null,"notation":[],"proof":"Write $b = a q$ and $c = b r$. Then $c = (a q) r = a (q r)$, so $a \\mid c$. □","provenance":{"context_package_id":null,"corpus_version":0,"created_at":"2026-09-10T03:49:19.239673Z","input_fingerprint":"ed191e2c602e59092a05ef88e6eacbb07ac6107241bb7950619535d6ca72fc8b","model":null,"producer":"ingest/v1","prompt_version":null,"provider":null},"section":"1.1","span":{"end":375,"pdf_page":1,"printed_page":1,"start":224},"statement":"If $a \\mid b$ and $b \\mid c$, then $a \\mid c$."}
{"chapter":"1","chapter_id":"dn-ch1","document_id":"dn","id":"dn-ch1-thm-1.2","kind":"theorem","label":"1.2","name":null,"notation":[],"proof":"Write $b = a q$ and $c = a r$. Then $b + c = a q + a r = a (q + r)$. □\n\n\n## 1.2 Consequences","provenance":{"context_package_id":null,"corpus_version":0,"created_at":"2026-09-10T03:49:19.239673Z","input_fingerprint":"ed191e2c602e59092a05ef88e6eacbb07ac6107241bb7950619535d6ca72fc8b","model":null,"producer":"ingest/v1","prompt_version":null,"provider":null},"section":"1.1","span":{"end":560,"pdf_page":1,"printed_page":1,"start":375},"statement":"If $a \\mid b$ and $a \\mid c$, then $a \\mid (b + c)$."}
{"chapter":"1","chapter_id":"dn-ch1","document_id":"dn","id":"dn-ch1-thm-1.3","kind":"theorem","label":"1.3","name":null,"notation":[],"proof":"By theorem 1.2, $a \\mid (b + c)$. Applying theorem 1.2 again to $b$ and $b + c$ gives $a \\mid (b + (b + c))$, which is the claim by associativity. □\n\n\n1.2.1 Show that $a \\mid 0$ for every $a$.\n\n1.2.2 Show that if $a \\mid b$ and $b \\mid a$ then $a = b$.","provenance":{"context_package_id":null,"corpus_version":0,"created_at":"2026-09-10T03:49:19.239673Z","input_fingerprint":"ed191e2c602e59092a05ef88e6eacbb07ac6107241bb7950619535d6ca72fc8b","model":null,"producer":"ingest/v1","prompt_version":null,"provider":null},"section":"1.2","span":{"end":848,"pdf_page":2,"printed_page":2,"start":560},"statement":"If $a \\mid b$ and $a \\mid c$, then $a \\mid (b + b + c)$."}
